00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
all right for this week's classic  interview the topic of Medicare for all  or some system that would get care to  everybody regardless of ability to pay  was popularized it was really sort of  mainstreamed by Bernie Sanders in 2016  many of the 2020 Democratic candidates  are now talking about this and we did a  neck an interview with a really  knowledgeable expert on the issue back  in 2012 okay this is how far ahead of  this thing we were in 2012 isn't even  early all right but from the point of  view of a show doing this stuff in depth  we were doing this 2012 and earlier and  in 2012 I interviewed dr. Carol Paris  and that's the topic and particularly  relevant today note the language that  was used in the interview with Carol  Paris compared to the language that  we're using now different language but  still talking about very similar ideas  let's get right to it dr. Carol Paris is  joining us she's a psychiatrist in  private practice in Maryland also a  member of physicians for a National  Health Program dr. Paris it's great to  talk to you I think a good place to  start will be to get your sense of why  you think a single-payer national health  care program would be would be a better  thing than we have now but also I'd be  curious to hear why some of your other  colleagues other doctors aren't  unanimous in that in that idea if it is  if it is beneficial as I believe you  think it is well thank you for having me  on the show yes I do think that it is  beneficial and there's lots of  statistics we can talk about or not to  support why it is beneficial both for  physicians and most importantly for  ninety five percent of the Americans in  terms of just economically advantageous  to them not to mention giving them  comprehensive coverage but why do I so I  think it's it's beneficial both from an  economic standpoint and and from a moral  ethical  you know healthcare is a human right  perspective but even if you you know  even if you don't approach it from that  perspective just from an economic  standpoint it is superior why do I think  other physicians aren't on board with it  well actually fifty nine percent of  physicians that were surveyed back in  2008 and remember that's even before the  Affordable Care Act you know 2009  legislation that said that they thought  that a national health program would be  superior and that was up 10 percent from  five years earlier that was published in  the annals of internal medicine and just  if you look at the numbers of membership  in physicians for a National Health  Program our membership increased by  three or four thousand physicians in  2009 simply because there was so much  more awareness of the problems with our  healthcare system even though there was  not a great deal of public media  awareness of single-payer as a an option  so let's talk about that a little bit I  mean I think on this program we talk a  lot from the patient point of view why a  single-payer system would would be  better but as a practicing physician I'm  curious what your thoughts would be  about well how would that allow you to  provide better care or would it would it  affect how would it affect the income of  the average physician I mean go into  that a little bit oh I'd be glad to I  got into this as I said I joined PHP in  2009 and that was after I had spent four  or five years with our state med the  Maryland State Medical Society and I was  very active in this state medical  society and I was quite proud of a piece  of legislation that I got was  instrumental in getting passed that was  designed to get the insurance companies  on one particular issue to play fair  and when it was all said and done when  the regulations were written they were  written by the insurance companies and  there's no fine for violating the law  and and it was at that point that I just  said you know there's there's just  something wrong with insurance companies  no matter what we do they will find a  workaround they will find a way to get  around any legislation that you pass and  that's what I got involved in in  physicians for a National Health Program  and started looking at putting a dollar  sign on what that would mean me and my  own practice and one of the things that  I came across was an article in Health  Affairs that suggests that the average  primary care physician the cost to the  average primary care physician is 68,000  so we're around sixty eight thousand  dollars a year per physician per year  just as the cost of dealing with  multiple insurance companies okay so  this takes into consideration if I  understand the average primary care  physician I don't know what numbers  you're using is it a slate of about  2,500 patients that they have kind of on  their roster I don't know what the  number of patients is this was published  in Health Affairs edition but the idea  would be that the average position with  an average patient load because of both  billing and timing put in to working  with insurance companies would stand to  save sixty eight thousand dollars if  that bureaucracy was eliminated that is  correct  okay now as far as as far as care is  concerned does it just follow that  simply by not having to deal with that  you're providing better care well I  think usually you have people working  and on the billing side so I don't know  that that's the argument but how would  it translate to better care well  actually the people that I have working  on the billing side are medical  assistants they could actually be doing  blood pressures and weights and action  things that would be good for my  patients rather than answering phones  and chasing prior authorizations so from  that perspective it would be good for  the patients plus if I don't if I'm  getting a raise  simply by not having to spend have so  much overhead go into dealing with  multiple insurance companies then I  might actually be able to see a couple  fewer patients a day and spend more time  with the patients that I'm actually  seeing which definitely translates into  better care I think if you ask the  average patient what their feeling is  about the amount of time that their  physician spends with them I don't think  you're often going to hear that that  patients feel like their doctor spends  as much time as he or she need or at  least from the person's perspective that  the patient feels is necessary and I  don't think that's because doctors don't  like to spend time with patients it's  because we're like hamsters in a cage  unless we see a volume of patients every  day we can't pay the bills we can't pay  our staff and have any money left over  for an income so on the last end of this  I guess my question would be about as  far as and this is probably a issue that  has multiple influences both the fear of  lawsuits but also what insurance  companies will and won't cover which is  that of necessary or unnecessary tests  that are often done we just did a piece  a couple of weeks ago about a number I  think it's 45 different tests that a  group of doctors came together and said  you know these are really overdone and  if people did them less you know in  specific conditions we would actually  eliminate a ton of spending at the same  time a lot of the reason that those  tests are being done is because doctors  both need to cover themselves and know  that if they're not done and documented  future procedures may not be covered so  talk a little bit about how the the  diagnosis and testing might change with  a single-payer health care  well the argument has made the doctors  do so much of the testing because  they're afraid of being sued right right  and so when you think about malpractice  and you look at what when you break a  malpractice award down the majority of  the money in any malpractice award is  for is to cover the patient for future  medical care hmm  well guess what that goes away if we had  a National Health Program because that's  already assured you get your Medicare  card the day you're born and you lose it  the day you die  so necessarily every malpractice award  is going to be significantly reduced if  we had a National Health Program which  then translates into a smaller premium  for your malpractice insurance because  the payouts are not what they used to be  which again translates into more income  for doctors and fewer malpractice claims  because the majority of the reason that  people pursue a malpractice claim in the  first place is because they need to be  assured of continued care and if they  don't have to be assured of continued  care then if it's one of those cases  where it really wasn't malpractice but  well but it's equivocal and the  patient's not is is going to have  expensive care for the rest of their  lives that goes away with the National  Health Program okay I wish we had more  time we've been speaking with dr. Carol  Paris she's a psychiatrist in private  practice in Maryland also a member of  physicians for a National Health Program  really great to speak to you and we'd  love to have you back again thank you  very much

